ev0 = pd.DataFrame(json.load(open(R + "/evaluator_results.json"))); rm = pd.read_csv(R + "/rmf.csv")
add("<div class='title'><h1>PathSnap: Map Matching and Route Reconstruction from Noisy GPS Using a Candidate Classifier and an HMM/Viterbi Decoder</h1><div class='au'>Team PathSnap (harish-rameshkumar, ManaswinHK, gopya3106, hanshalnh)</div><div class='af'>UE24CS352A Machine Learning, Department of Computer Science and Engineering, PES University RR Campus. Code: github.com/harish-rameshkumar/pathsnap</div></div>")
add(f"<div class='abs'><i>Abstract</i>&mdash;GPS fixes from vehicles are noisy and often sparse, so a fix can lie on a parallel street or off the road network. We reconstruct the travelled route by matching each fix to a road segment (arc). For every fix we generate up to 12 candidate arcs within 80 m, describe each candidate with 19 geometric and topological features, score it with a supervised classifier, and decode a connected route with a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) and the Viterbi algorithm. We compare the classifiers in group-aware cross-validation (folds split by trip) and tune the best boosted-tree model with Bayesian optimisation. On the 10 held-out evaluator trips, the final system reaches a pointwise accuracy of {ev0.hmm_pointwise_accuracy.mean():.3f} (classifier alone: {ev0.clf_pointwise_accuracy.mean():.3f}), and reduces the Route Mismatch Fraction from {rm[rm.partition=='evaluator_test'].clf_rmf.mean():.3f} to {rm[rm.partition=='evaluator_test'].hmm_rmf.mean():.3f}. The system is deployed as a FastAPI service and a web interface on two laptops.<br><i>Index Terms</i>&mdash;map matching, GPS trajectories, hidden Markov model, Viterbi decoding, gradient boosting, Bayesian optimisation.</div>")
add("<div class='body'>")
add("<h2>Introduction</h2><p>GPS positions suffer from urban canyons, multipath, dropouts and low sampling rates, so a vehicle can appear to move onto a parallel street or jump across buildings. Map matching infers the true path through a road network from such noisy, possibly sparse, measurements. We follow a two-stage design: (i) for each fix, a classifier gives the probability that each nearby road segment is the correct one; (ii) an HMM combines these probabilities with road-network connectivity to choose the most coherent sequence of segments.</p><p>The paper is organised as follows. Section II reviews related work. Section III describes the data and exploration. Section IV compares the classifiers, Section V tunes the selected model, and Section VI describes the HMM decoder and its results. Sections VII and VIII cover map visualisation, the API, the user interface and the two-laptop deployment test, and Section IX states limitations.</p>")
add("<h2>Related Work</h2><p>The HMM approach of Newson and Krumm [1] treats the candidate road segments of each fix as hidden states, uses a measurement-noise model for emission probabilities and the difference between straight-line and along-road distance for transition probabilities, and finds the best path with Viterbi decoding [3]. Fiedler et al. [2] discuss the cost of this global approach on large datasets and propose a faster shortest-path-based method, evaluated with the Route Mismatch Fraction (RMF) introduced in [1]. Our decoder keeps the transition model of [1] but replaces the Gaussian emission model by a <i>learned</i> emission probability: the output of a classifier trained on labelled candidates. Gradient-boosted trees [4] were selected as the classifier, and their hyperparameters were optimised with the Tree-structured Parzen Estimator [6] as implemented in Optuna [5].</p>")
# ---- 2 EDA
add("<h2>Dataset understanding and EDA</h2><h3>Preprocessing</h3><ul><li><b>Partitions:</b> 80 training trips (fitting, CV, Optuna, HMM tuning with out-of-fold probabilities), 10 student-test trips (development comparisons only), evaluator trips never used.</li><li><b>Parsing/validation:</b> .track = lon, lat, t (no header); rows with NaN/out-of-range coordinates dropped, sorted by time (none were needed, see Section III-B). Arc id = row index in .arcs; .route is a list of arc ids.</li><li><b>Projection:</b> every trip has its own road network somewhere in the world, so each trip is projected to a local equirectangular plane in metres around its own centroid; city-scale distortion is far below GPS noise.</li><li><b>Reference labels per fix:</b> .route has no per-fix labels, so fixes are aligned to the route by a monotone dynamic programme (non-decreasing route index, forward jumps up to 25 arcs, squared distance cost clipped at 100 m). Labels are used only for training/evaluation.</li><li><b>Excluded from fitting:</b> training trips whose reference route disagrees with the GPS (candidate miss rate > 30 %): trips 00000033 (66 %) and 00000054 (90 %). They remain in the inventory.</li></ul>")
add("<h3>Missing / invalid value analysis (all raw .track rows; partitions as stated)</h3>" + table(miss.round(4).values.tolist(), list(miss.columns), "Missing and invalid value summary of the raw GPS files, by partition") + "<p>No NaN rows, no out-of-order timestamps and no isolated road nodes were found in any partition. The real irregularity is in sampling: fixes are nominally 1 Hz but gaps exist; 40 of 80 training trips and 2 of 10 student trips contain gaps above 10 s (largest training gap 153,064 s, largest student gap 210 s). Hence the HMM restarts its chain after a gap of gap_s seconds. The road networks are not single connected components (median 20 components per training trip), which is why unreachable transitions must be handled in the decoder.</p>" + img("sampling_intervals.png", "75%", "Time step between consecutive GPS fixes (left, log scale) and fraction of exactly 1 s steps per trip (right); all 90 trips."))
add("<h3>Patterns observed</h3>" + img("dataset_overview.png", "100%", "<i>Partition: all 90 trips (train + student).</i> Trip lengths range from a few hundred to 19,313 fixes; trips are worldwide (lower-right panel); road networks range from 299 to 307,392 arcs.") + img("gps_trajectories_train.png", "100%", "<i>Partition: training.</i> GPS trajectories of six training trips.") + img("road_network_gps_route.png", "100%", "<i>Partitions: train (left, trip 00000034) and student-test (right, trip 00000040).</i> Road network (grey), reference route (green) and GPS fixes (blue). The GPS sits within roughly 5-10 m (median) of the reference route, with 99th percentile ~25 m, so an 80 m candidate radius contains the true arc for >99 % of fixes on reliable trips."))
add("<h3>Feature engineering and why</h3><p>Candidates: top 12 arcs within 80 m by true point-to-segment distance. Features (computed from GPS + network only):</p><ul><li><code>d, d_rel, d_ratio, rank, n_cand</code> – how close the arc is, absolutely and relative to the best candidate (parallel roads are separated by relative distance).</li><li><code>d_smooth, d_smooth_rel</code> – mean distance of fixes t-2..t+2 to the arc: robust to single-fix GPS noise.</li><li><code>cos_head, cos_head_wide</code> – cosine between arc direction and the smoothed motion direction (two windows): separates the two directions of the same road and crossing roads; set to 0 when speed is below ~3 m/step.</li><li><code>speed, dt_prev</code> – motion state/gap context.</li><li><code>frac, arc_len, out_deg, in_deg</code> – projection fraction, segment length, junction structure.</li><li><code>supp_prev, supp_next, same_prev_cand, same_next_cand</code> – whether the arc connects to, or is also a candidate at, the neighbouring fixes (local topological support).</li></ul><p>Candidate miss rate (reference arc absent from candidates), student-test: " + f"{inv[inv.part=='student_test'].cand_miss.mean()*100:.2f}% mean, {inv[inv.part=='student_test'].cand_miss.max()*100:.2f}% max.</p>")
add("<h3>Candidate generation analysis</h3>" + img("candidates_overview.png", "100%", "<i>Partitions: 78 reliable training trips for panels 1-5; all 90 trips for the per-trip miss rate.</i> Candidates are arcs within 80 m, capped at 12 per fix (mean 8.5; 0.3% of fixes have none). The reference arc is typically within ~10 m, but it is the <i>nearest</i> arc for only about 57% of fixes; rank 1 is often the opposite-direction arc of the same road at nearly the same distance, which is why heading agreement and smoothed distance are used as features. Trips 33 and 54 (red) have reference routes that disagree with the GPS and were excluded from fitting.") + img("candidates_examples.png", "100%", "Four example fixes from student-test trip 00000056: GPS fix (blue star), 80 m search circle (dashed), all candidate arcs (grey) and the reference arc (red)."))
add("<h3>Folium map of a trip</h3><p>Interactive map: <code>results/figures/trip_00000034_map.html</code> (GPS fixes and reference route, training trip 00000034); prediction maps in Section VII.</p>")
# ---- 3 models
add("<h2>Model experimentation and comparison</h2><p>All models are scikit-learn [7] or LightGBM [4] implementations. Protocol: 392,657 candidate rows (25 % of observations of 78 reliable training trips, 11.6 % positive), 5-fold GroupKFold by trip (no trip split across folds), random seed 42. Final student-test evaluation on the 10 student trips (all candidates). Macro-F1 is over the two classes at threshold 0.5; Top-1 accuracy is observation-level (highest-scoring candidate vs reference arc, all fixes counted, fixes without candidates counted wrong); route continuity = fraction of predicted arc changes that are physically adjacent in the network.</p>")
rows = [[r["model"], r["family"], CONFIG[r["model"]][0], f4(r["cv_macro_f1"]) + "±" + f4(r["cv_macro_f1_std"]), f4(r["top1_obs"]), f4(r["continuity"]), f4(r["macro_f1"]), f4(r["log_loss"]), f"{r['infer_sec_total']:.2f} s"] for r in mc]
add("<h3>Overall comparison</h3>" + table(rows, ["Model", "Algorithm family", "Key configuration", "CV macro-F1", "Top-1 acc (student)", "Route continuity (student, classifier only)", "Student macro-F1", "Student log loss", "Inference time (10 student trips)"], "Model comparison: 5-fold grouped CV on training trips and evaluation on the 10 student-test trips") + "<p>Log loss applies to all models (all produce probabilities). Inference time is total predict_proba time over all 10 student trips.</p>")
add("<h3>Per-model analysis</h3>")
